-- #1111 A-2.10 BYSTANDER FIXTURE — "feature + UNRELATED code still behaves",
-- which is the shape `AGENTS.md` requires of a change that alters what a
-- PROGRAM-WIDE comparison answers, as opposed to "feature works".
--
-- A-2.10 makes six type comparisons read the module identity on a type head.
-- The fixtures that prove the FEATURE are the drained #1208/#1209 repros — each
-- asserts that two DIFFERENT types sharing a name are now REJECTED.  This one
-- asserts the other half, which no drained repro can: that a module which
-- shadows nothing is inferred exactly as it would be with no collision anywhere
-- in the graph.
--
-- The graph DOES contain a collision: `pa.mdk` and `pz.mdk` both declare a type
-- `Twin` at arity 1, with different payloads.  Only their unrelated FUNCTIONS
-- are imported here, so both `Twin`s are registered into the cross-module
-- universe and neither is nameable from this file.  NOTHING BELOW MENTIONS
-- `Twin`.
--
-- 🔬 COUNTERFACTUAL RUN AGAINST THIS HEADER'S OWN CLAIM (the check `AGENTS.md`
-- asks for, because a header that merely ASSERTS coverage is the commonest
-- prose defect in this arc).  The claim is "this fixture is sensitive to the
-- A-2.10 mechanism".  Deleting the mechanism — reverting `unifyN`'s arm to the
-- name-only `if a == b` — leaves this fixture GREEN.  That is stated rather
-- than hidden, and it is the CORRECT result for a bystander: its job is to fail
-- when the mechanism BREAKS UNRELATED CODE, and a fixture that also moved when
-- the mechanism was removed would be a feature test wearing a bystander's
-- header.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT THAT COUNTERFACTUAL DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH — narrowed 2026-08-04,
-- because the first wording claimed more than the experiment it cited.  Reverting
-- `unifyN` REMOVES THE READER, which trivially greens everything downstream of it;
-- that is a sound test of "this is not a feature test", and nothing more.  It is
-- NOT a test of a WRONG SUPPLY: no arm of it hands a head the wrong identity, so
-- it cannot tell a correct supply from a broken one.
--
-- The header used to say the fixture was sensitive to wrong supply "by
-- construction rather than by assertion" — that was an assertion.  The bound
-- that IS honest: every binding below names a head that must acquire an identity
-- (`own`'s types by import, the prelude's by prepending, the builtins by their
-- own mint), so a supply defect REACHING ONE OF THESE HEADS drops its binding
-- from the golden.  Whether any given supply defect reaches them is not
-- something this fixture measures, and one that provably does not is the
-- loader's dual-spelling defect — an entry BELOW its project root gets two
-- `OriginModule` ids for one file, which this flat fixture directory cannot
-- express at all (that leg lives in
-- `test/diff_compiler_check_cli_modules.sh`'s `A-2.10/nested-project-*`).  The
-- collision two modules over is what makes a wrong supply a live risk here
-- rather than a hypothetical; it is not proof that this golden would catch one.
import own.{Parcel(..), unParcel, Weigh(..), weigh, Sack}
import pa.{pa1} -- ONLY the function; never pa's Twin
import pz.{pz1} -- ONLY the function; never pz's Twin

crossModuleType : Parcel Int
crossModuleType = MkParcel 5

crossModuleAlias : Sack -> Int
crossModuleAlias p = unParcel p

crossModuleDispatch : Int
crossModuleDispatch = weigh (unParcel crossModuleType)

externSourced : Bool
externSourced = stringLength "xy" < 2

preludeTypes : Option (Result String Int)
preludeTypes = Some (Ok 1)

builtinHeads : (List Int, String)
builtinHeads = ([1, 2], "s")

throughCollidingModules : Int
throughCollidingModules = pa1 (pz1 3)
